er-field/id #uuid "6d6789d2-b01f-5e23-bdd6-b8f952eb31a6" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186755781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22880610-877f-51d5-bc3a-7c41ecfc0b79" :question/math-academy-id "q-338006" :question/problem "Select all homogeneous second-order linear differential equations.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+xy=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=4x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=4x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$"}] [17592186311909 {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152274}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9119a1e-cd12-515d-b0cd-05956c355688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186755785} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1594d2-0413-504b-8ff5-be5e487d2c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186755786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9119a1e-cd12-515d-b0cd-05956c355688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text